finding: Re-review of 0f65f31a: the original two findings are addressed in their intended shape. MaterialSource is a narrow borrowed renderer-input seam (table, alpha pipeline inputs, optional CPU sphere), not a renamed component ownership mode: it allocates/releases no rows, knows no component schema or membership, and Sprite owns binding, lifetime and publication. Keeping the standard mesh pipeline/shadows/transparent phase is appropriate. The origin-centred CPU sphere restores ordinary Sprite sorting and conservative rejection without a mirror. Both new regressions pass independently. Two amendment defects remain: 1. A sourced material still exposes an operative MaterialAssets allocator whose handles select another owner's rows. materialType's create still constructs MaterialAssets even when definition.source exists. MaterialAssets.add returns the same {type, material} namespace used by the external source, but rendering and CPU alpha read exclusively from source. material-source.node.ts explicitly adds a green asset, observes that rendering ignores it, and treats this as success. Attaching that returned handle would select an unrelated external row (or a row the source does not own), not the added green asset. This violates the material handle contract and makes ownership ambiguous at the public seam. Smallest fix: refuse constructing/accessing the asset allocator for a type with an external source (before allocating or registering any asset rows), and replace the ignored-write assertion with a refusal assertion. This is an invalid allocator/type combination, not a second row mode; MaterialAssets need not regain component state or branching in each mutator. 2. Sprite's CPU bounds read the consuming eid's Sprite/Anchor rather than the selected material row's owner. table.ts cullBounds(eid,out) accesses sprite.size and Anchor directly at eid, whereas GPU publication indexes parameters by MeshInstance.material. A mesh sharing a live Sprite row need not hold Sprite. Independent dynamically loaded GPU-app probe: owner size [10,10], Blend, shared mesh using the same material row; owner CPU radius = 7.071067810058594, shared CPU radius = 0, sameRow = true. GPU geometry/bounds remain the owner's full quad. Thus a shared Blend sprite reaching into a view can be CPU-culled by a zero/undefined/stale radius; another Sprite's row or Anchor can also disagree. The generic sharing test has no bounds callback and misses this. Smallest fix: derive CPU bounds from the selected row's uploaded size/anchor/flags (or resolve the row's owner), preserving origin-centred sorting and row lifetime, and add a shared live Sprite-row edge-of-frustum regression with a consumer lacking Sprite. If sharing is deliberately unsupported, it must instead be explicitly refused before drawing, but that would narrow the prior requirement. Independent examination: amendment diff and new source/renderer paths, MaterialAssets construction and handle namespace, source proof, sprite table/publication/membership, transparent regressions, floor/isolation fixture updates. Ran transparency, bulk, material-source, culling, floor and compile: 22 tests pass. Independently confirmed 0 setAt and 0 mesh field setters, 28 Sprite native pipelines, 7/7 Sprite storage bindings and 3 publication compute bindings. Floor adapter identifies Apple M4 Max / Metal; floor uploads now 8812 bytes, 9 compute passes and 13 dispatches. Extra pipeline remains an actual Sprite publication job. No source edits; working tree clean. Notes/remaining uncertainty: names, defaults and migration replacements retain the prior review's assessment. No independent frame-oracle rerun, full isolation/disposal run or memory-retention measurement in this re-review. Source table lifetime is owner-defined; sharing after owner removal is not promised. The sharing failure above occurs while the owner remains alive, not after invalidation. Initial ad-hoc static-import probes lacked TGSL metadata; repeated with Shallot's Bun transform registered before dynamic imports and obtained the concrete bounds values above.
